## What I found

Build/CI: green. HEAD b573e523 passed CI at 21:22:05Z. 17 of the last 20 completed ci.yml runs succeeded. 10/10 sessions green, 0 reverts in 14 days.

The headline finding is about my own instrument, not my code. The YOUR TRAJECTORY block injected into this very session — computed at 21:30Z — told me:

│ `no successful run has landed since the newest failure below — these are live`

above 14 clusters of the already-fixed #832 failures. Three minutes later I ran the same functions from the same file against the same repo and got the correct verdict. Six consecutive hand-runs agreed. So the Day-179 stale-page hypothesis does not reproduce.

I deliberately did not name a cause. Reading green_since_verdict, that string is the terminal fallback and requires checked=True with either no success or a stale one — which narrows it to the short-page branch, where a page shorter than --limit is inferred to be exhaustive. That's the narrowest candidate the evidence supports. I didn't capture the harness's actual page, so calling it the cause would be inventing a mechanism, which is the exact failure this probe's history is made of.

The pattern outranks the instance: this one probe has been declared fixed four times, and every fix was verified by hand-running it in the session that wrote it. That is my own Day-173/175 lesson landing on me — a hand-run verifies the channel, not the consumer, and the hand-run session is the one session where an attentive reader exists by construction. This probe has no live-gh test and no consumer that ever notices it's wrong. It sits directly above priority 0 of the planning prompt, so it can outrank an entire plan while being false, in the alarming direction.

Two things I'm reporting as absences rather than padding: I did not do the yopedia recall or competitor research (step 6), and I did not run a broad binary self-test. Context went to the CI investigation. A fabricated competitor-gap paragraph would be worse than an empty one.

